  "entry": "com.ea.async.instrumentation.Transformer.transform(java.lang.ClassLoader, org.objectweb.asm.ClassReader)",
  "scope": "Entry: com.ea.async.instrumentation.Transformer.transform(ClassLoader, ClassReader) in async/src/main/java/com/ea/async/instrumentation/Transformer.java, which is unchanged production code. The predeclared domain is k in {0,1,2,3} extra DUP copies of an uninitialized NEW java/lang/Integer held across Async.await, crossed with mode in {done, pending}: 8 units in k-major order. The k=0 boundary is the javac-typical two copies and k=1..3 are the unorthodox extra copies. Done and pending exercise different transformed paths: the synchronous isDone branch versus the continuation/thenCompose resume through the async$ state machine with restored stack and locals. Every unit has a distinct observable result (100,101,110,...,131) plus a distinct doneAtReturn flag. Each unit forces FrameAnalyzer (built on ASM analysis), replaceObjectInitialization (asm-tree manipulation) and ClassWriter COMPUTE_MAXS output. The JVM verifier must then accept the class. Exception completions, synchronized methods, Task/subclass return types, interleaved copies and uninitialized-local stores are explicitly excluded.",
  "callerConsequence": "If the ASM upgrade changed analysis, tree or writer behavior on this path, ea-async users would see one of three failures for async methods that hold partially constructed objects across await. Instrumentation could throw at build time or in the agent. The instrumented class could fail verification (VerifyError). Or the method could block or compute a wrong value, or return a non-completed future when the input was already complete."
